<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="0f5a09a1-60d5-4059-8bae-075eaf192191" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerSampleDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>The SchedulerConsole project shows a regular .NET Console application using DemiCode Scheduler,
                reading in the configuration from a configuration file. The application uses a five-second
                interval timer in order to issue messages to the console.</para>
    </introduction>

   
            
            
            
                
            
            
<section>
  <title>Prerequisites</title>
  <content>
    <para>Navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</legacyBold> folder. Unzip the <legacyBold>SchedulerSamples.zip</legacyBold>
                    in the Samples folder.</para>
  </content>
</section>
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using the Command Prompt</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open the Visual Studio 2005 Command Prompt window and navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler
                                1.1\Samples</legacyBold> folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Type <legacyBold>msbuild SchedulerConsole.sln</legacyBold> at the command line.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>
       
                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To build the sample using Visual Studio</title>
  <content>
    <list class="ordered">
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Open Windows Explorer and navigate to the <legacyBold>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1\Samples</legacyBold>
                            folder.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            Double-click the <legacyBold>SchedulerConsole.sln</legacyBold> to open the file in Visual Studio.</para>
                    </listItem>
                    <listItem>
                        <para>
                            In the <legacyBold>Build</legacyBold> menu, select <legacyBold>Build Solution</legacyBold>.</para>
                    </listItem>
                </list>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>To run the sample</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        Follow these steps to run the sample console application:
                        <list class="ordered">
                            <listItem>Open a Command Prompt and navigate to the directory that contains the new
                                executable. <para/>
                                This will usually be <legacyBold>Samples\SchedulerConsole\bin\Debug</legacyBold></listItem>
                            <listItem>Run the application by typing:
                            <code language="other">
                            SchedulerConsole.exe [Enter]
                            </code>
                            </listItem>
                            <listItem>Let the application run for at least 5 seconds before pressing [ENTER]. Messages from the scheduler should appear in the console.</listItem>
                        </list>
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

                
            
            
<section>
  <title>Requirements</title>
  <content>
    <para>DemiCode Scheduler 1.1 and .NET Framework 2.0.</para>
  </content>
</section>

            
            
<section>
  <title>Remarks</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        This sample demonstrates the use of the .NET configuration system to configure our
                        scheduler. Also the ability to hook event handlers to the scheduler, either at the
                        top level (recieving events from all schedules) or at a specific schedule.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        With a few lines of code we are able to create a simple application taking advantage of the scheduling capabilities in DemiCode Scheduler.
                        <code language="c#">
static void Main()
{
    // 1. Create default scheduler from configuration
    using (Scheduler _scheduler = SchedulerFactory.CreateFromConfig("scheduling"))
    {
        // 2. Hook up to events
        _scheduler.ScheduledAlarm += new EventHandler&lt;AlarmEventArgs&gt;(_scheduler_ScheduledAlarm);
        _scheduler[&quot;FiveSecInterval&quot;].Alarm += new EventHandler&lt;AlarmEventArgs&gt;(fiveSecInterval_Alarm);

        // 3. Start the engine
        _scheduler.Start();

        // 4. Wait for user input
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;Press [ENTER] to exit&quot;);
        Console.ReadLine();

        // 5. Stop the engine
        _scheduler.Stop();
    }
}                        </code>
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        One thing to pay attention to is how we <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false" autoUpgrade="true">M:DemiCode.Scheduling.Scheduler.Dispose</codeEntityReference>
                        our scheduler instance with the simple <legacyBold>using</legacyBold> syntax. This is important since we're holding on to a valuable timer
                        resource.
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>

            
        
    

